I know, but I had to hold it while you went to the bathroom.

768
00:32:24.150 --> 00:32:28.530
Yes, I thought I was going to play guitar, but then I broke my back on that trip and I

769
00:32:28.530 --> 00:32:30.070
bruised my elbow.

770
00:32:30.390 --> 00:32:33.390
And by the time I was healed, I was over the guitar thing.

771
00:32:33.550 --> 00:32:39.730
I wish I played the guitar and I learned piano growing up, but I quit too early.

772
00:32:39.730 --> 00:32:41.670
So no, no musical instruments.

773
00:32:41.930 --> 00:32:42.990
It's never too late, lady.

774
00:32:43.390 --> 00:32:44.150
It's never too late.

775
00:32:44.150 --> 00:32:45.290
I feel like it's too late.

776
00:32:45.330 --> 00:32:46.270
Okay, it's too late.

777
00:32:46.430 --> 00:32:46.850
Forget it.

778
00:32:46.870 --> 00:32:47.390
It's too late for me.

779
00:32:48.910 --> 00:32:50.830
And do you have a favorite book you've read?

780
00:32:52.330 --> 00:32:53.450
This is a tough one.

781
00:32:53.670 --> 00:32:58.050
This has got to be such a difficult question for you because I feel like there's at least

782
00:32:58.050 --> 00:33:01.630
once a month where you leave me a message where you're like, Angela, this book changed

783
00:33:01.630 --> 00:33:02.090
my life.

784
00:33:02.190 --> 00:33:02.550
I know.

785
00:33:02.650 --> 00:33:03.010
I know.

786
00:33:03.010 --> 00:33:05.350
I love every book almost that I read.

787
00:33:05.350 --> 00:33:11.150
But I'll say my favorite book that I read last year that keeps coming up for me and

788
00:33:11.150 --> 00:33:13.650
has actually impacted my life.

789
00:33:13.710 --> 00:33:20.910
You were talking about some of the work that I do for the unhoused community, which I have

790
00:33:20.910 --> 00:33:26.370
done for a long time, but was reinvigorated after reading Demon Copperhead.

791
00:33:27.650 --> 00:33:29.830
I absolutely loved that book.

792
00:33:30.470 --> 00:33:37.730
And within that book, it's about the struggles of this young man who ends up losing both of

793
00:33:37.730 --> 00:33:40.890
his parents, and he's put into foster care, and he's hungry a lot.

794
00:33:41.250 --> 00:33:45.930
And along the way in his life, it gets emotional, even just thinking about it like he's such a

795
00:33:45.930 --> 00:33:46.850
real person to me.

796
00:33:47.310 --> 00:33:53.550
Along the way, there are just every once in a while, someone's kind to him and just creates

797
00:33:53.550 --> 00:33:54.950
a human moment with him.

798
00:33:54.950 --> 00:33:58.750
That's the thing you shared with me is like giving someone a moment of humanity.

799
00:33:59.130 --> 00:33:59.330
Yeah.

800
00:33:59.630 --> 00:34:07.410
And so that became sort of a goal of mine is just there's a lot of unhoused people in Los

801
00:34:07.410 --> 00:34:19.670
Angeles, and just the idea that maybe for a brief moment, if they encounter me, I just

802
00:34:20.470 --> 00:34:24.390
create that moment of kindness and offer help and support.

803
00:34:24.390 --> 00:34:28.870
Along with working with some organizations that help with housing and outreach and clothing

804
00:34:28.870 --> 00:34:29.550
and things like that.

805
00:34:29.710 --> 00:34:33.210
But for me, it's a very personal one-on-one thing.

806
00:34:33.330 --> 00:34:40.090
And it's really due to that book of just the moments when in the book, someone would offer

807
00:34:40.090 --> 00:34:40.730
him food.

808
00:34:42.250 --> 00:34:46.850
And I would just be so happy that someone was kind to him.

809
00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:47.270
Yeah.

810
00:34:47.630 --> 00:34:50.390
And I felt it so deeply.

811
00:34:50.390 --> 00:34:53.610
I thought, oh my God, I just, I want to be that for somebody if I can.

812
00:34:53.910 --> 00:34:54.150
Right.

813
00:34:54.150 --> 00:34:56.770
So that book just is a great book.

814
00:34:56.770 --> 00:34:57.990
It's a great story.

815
00:34:58.190 --> 00:35:03.090
And if I'm making it sound heavy, one of the amazing things about the narrator of the book,

816
00:35:03.210 --> 00:35:08.190
who is the boy, is he's funny and he is hopeful.

817
00:35:08.190 --> 00:35:12.870
And so it's just a really powerful book and entertaining and easy to read.

818
00:35:13.070 --> 00:35:14.310
So I loved it.

819
00:35:14.410 --> 00:35:17.570
And now I am reading another of her books.

820
00:35:17.710 --> 00:35:18.610
Barbara Kingsoliver?

821
00:35:18.810 --> 00:35:19.250
Yes.

822
00:35:19.250 --> 00:35:19.490
Yeah.

823
00:35:19.610 --> 00:35:25.590
I am reading The Poisonwood Bible, which is also just great so far.

824
00:35:25.710 --> 00:35:28.510
I mean, I'm 40 pages in, but very excited.

825
00:35:29.130 --> 00:35:29.470
Great.

826
00:35:29.770 --> 00:35:33.370
Well, I think that's fantastic recommendation, you guys out there listening.

827
00:35:33.670 --> 00:35:34.950
We all need to read it.

828
00:35:35.550 --> 00:35:36.670
Yeah, it's great.

829
00:35:37.130 --> 00:35:37.610
All right.

830
00:35:38.130 --> 00:35:42.270
One of my favorite questions whenever we have a guest, because I'm just so curious about

831
00:35:42.270 --> 00:35:45.490
where people love to go in the world.

832
00:35:46.010 --> 00:35:48.910
What's a place you've been to that you absolutely loved?

833
00:35:49.150 --> 00:35:49.470
All right.

834
00:35:49.510 --> 00:35:52.530
I'm going to divide this into domestically and internationally.

835
00:35:52.970 --> 00:35:53.830
Oh, great.

836
00:35:55.650 --> 00:35:58.510
Domestically, Oklahoma City, and Chicago.

837
00:35:59.530 --> 00:36:03.090
Last year, I got to go to both of those cities for different projects.

838
00:36:03.090 --> 00:36:05.870
And you got to really live in both of those cities.

839
00:36:06.110 --> 00:36:06.210
Yes.

840
00:36:06.210 --> 00:36:10.630
Like, you had an Airbnb, and you lived there, and you had your market, and you met people,

841
00:36:10.850 --> 00:36:11.730
and your cafes.

842
00:36:12.150 --> 00:36:12.510
Yes.

843
00:36:12.830 --> 00:36:16.730
I absolutely fell in love with Oklahoma City and the people of Oklahoma City.

844
00:36:16.930 --> 00:36:24.070
Their crazy, wackadoodle weather, where it's like an ice storm, and then 36 hours later,

845
00:36:24.070 --> 00:36:25.550
it's 78 degrees.

846
00:36:25.570 --> 00:36:27.310
I was like, what's happening here?

847
00:36:27.310 --> 00:36:30.610
And there's, like, tornado sirens going off.

848
00:36:30.750 --> 00:36:34.150
And also, the people locally, they're like, yep, we love it.

849
00:36:34.330 --> 00:36:34.530
Yeah.

850
00:36:34.630 --> 00:36:35.970
And I'm like, I get it.

851
00:36:36.070 --> 00:36:36.610
I don't know.

852
00:36:36.750 --> 00:36:37.710
There's something about it.

853
00:36:37.790 --> 00:36:42.830
You're like, and maybe it's because we live in the perpetual sunshine of Los Angeles.

854
00:36:43.230 --> 00:36:52.190
But this idea that the weather could be literally anything tomorrow was very cool and exciting and weird.

855
00:36:52.610 --> 00:36:52.930
Exactly.

856
00:36:53.230 --> 00:36:58.010
And, you know, my family in North Texas is kind of in that same corridor that Oklahoma City is in.

857
00:36:58.110 --> 00:37:03.230
And it brings community together because you're like, yes, a bunch of trees are coming down because of this wind.

858
00:37:03.370 --> 00:37:04.370
Okay, everybody, let's see.

859
00:37:04.410 --> 00:37:07.970
And then, you know, all of a sudden, there's a heat wave, and then it's like, pray for rain.

860
00:37:08.110 --> 00:37:09.790
And it's like, you know, you just never know.

861
00:37:10.070 --> 00:37:10.390
Yes.

862
00:37:10.990 --> 00:37:12.510
But also, the food.

863
00:37:13.090 --> 00:37:14.230
The food was so good.

864
00:37:14.430 --> 00:37:15.830
Everywhere I went to eat was so good.

865
00:37:15.970 --> 00:37:21.430
There was, like, this little taco place near our Airbnb that, I mean, we ordered from constantly.

866
00:37:21.430 --> 00:37:23.230
And this ramen place that was so good.

867
00:37:23.310 --> 00:37:26.250
But all the restaurants, everywhere we ate was great.

868
00:37:27.110 --> 00:37:32.310
Lee had to go back recently, and I was so jealous because I wanted to go with him.

869
00:37:32.410 --> 00:37:35.770
He had to do a screening of the movie for the crew.

870
00:37:36.370 --> 00:37:37.190
New Year's Rev.

871
00:37:37.490 --> 00:37:38.130
New Year's Rev.

872
00:37:38.250 --> 00:37:40.490
It's coming out this year, although I think the name is changing.

873
00:37:40.750 --> 00:37:41.190
Oh, yeah?

874
00:37:41.410 --> 00:37:41.570
Yeah.

875
00:37:41.830 --> 00:37:43.590
Once it's final, we can talk about it.

876
00:37:43.690 --> 00:37:48.030
But, yeah, he did a screening for the crew, and I was like, oh, my God, are you going to eat here?

877
00:37:48.170 --> 00:37:48.530
Are you going to eat?

878
00:37:48.570 --> 00:37:49.930
He was only there for two days.

879
00:37:51.430 --> 00:37:52.590
Because the food was so good.

880
00:37:53.010 --> 00:37:55.810
And then, of course, I have gone on and on about Chicago.

881
00:37:55.970 --> 00:37:58.090
I just fell in love with that city as well.

882
00:37:58.510 --> 00:37:59.370
Love Chicago.

883
00:37:59.770 --> 00:38:02.470
So those are my domestic loves.

884
00:38:02.850 --> 00:38:06.890
And then, internationally, I loved Copenhagen.

885
00:38:07.110 --> 00:38:08.730
Tell me about Copenhagen.

886
00:38:08.730 --> 00:38:09.490
I've never been.

887
00:38:09.730 --> 00:38:11.790
It is such a charming city.

888
00:38:11.790 --> 00:38:13.270
We went as a family.

889
00:38:13.610 --> 00:38:22.290
And in the middle of the city, they have an amusement park called Tivoli Gardens, which, Angela, you would love because it's also like a botanical gardens.

890
00:38:22.290 --> 00:38:28.130
And you can buy a ticket just to walk around, or you can buy a ticket that includes rides.

891
00:38:28.430 --> 00:38:30.470
So I bought the walk-around ticket.

892
00:38:31.130 --> 00:38:33.330
Lee and the kids bought the one for rides.

893
00:38:34.150 --> 00:38:44.630
They've got this old roller coaster that is, like, so old that there's a man that sits in the back and does a break for when you go around turns.

894
00:38:44.810 --> 00:38:45.450
Oh, my gosh.

895
00:38:45.450 --> 00:38:46.190
Isn't that crazy?

896
00:38:46.190 --> 00:38:48.370
See, I hope he's paying attention that day.

897
00:38:48.630 --> 00:38:50.590
Oh, my gosh.

898
00:38:50.890 --> 00:38:53.790
But there's also, like, a loop-de-loop, like, a more modern roller coaster.

899
00:38:53.910 --> 00:38:54.450
It's great.

900
00:38:54.550 --> 00:39:00.690
I guess it's supposedly the amusement park that Disneyland was originally based on, and I get it.

901
00:39:01.050 --> 00:39:02.790
But it's just super cute.

902
00:39:02.790 --> 00:39:05.170
But we also went to a soccer game there.

903
00:39:05.350 --> 00:39:06.890
A football, excuse me.

904
00:39:07.010 --> 00:39:07.930
Yeah, a football game.

905
00:39:07.970 --> 00:39:09.290
Mm-hmm, which was really cool.

906
00:39:09.290 --> 00:39:16.550
And we also rented, like, a little electric boat and brought a picnic dinner out on the canals, and we, like, putted around the canals.

907
00:39:16.650 --> 00:39:17.990
But it was great.

908
00:39:17.990 --> 00:39:20.210
It's a great walking city.

909
00:39:20.490 --> 00:39:21.910
It was just cool.

910
00:39:21.910 --> 00:39:23.410
And, again, had great food.

911
00:39:23.570 --> 00:39:25.930
If you have good food, I'm going to come to your city.

912
00:39:26.310 --> 00:39:27.110
Jenna's going to visit.

913
00:39:27.790 --> 00:39:28.230
Exactly.

914
00:39:28.410 --> 00:39:29.850
So tell her where to eat.

915
00:39:29.950 --> 00:39:30.590
She'll be there.

916
00:39:32.330 --> 00:39:34.130
Okay, well, I love those answers.

917
00:39:34.390 --> 00:39:38.130
And number four is, what do you like to do on the weekends?

918
00:39:39.290 --> 00:39:45.390
Well, I run a small taxi service for two very demanding clients every weekend.

919
00:39:45.950 --> 00:39:47.710
Your clients are how old?

920
00:39:47.850 --> 00:39:49.370
They are 14 and 11.

921
00:39:49.610 --> 00:39:49.870
Okay.

922
00:39:49.990 --> 00:39:51.610
They have a lot of extracurriculars.

923
00:39:52.450 --> 00:39:59.810
So truly, when I say I run a car service, you know, I do the scheduling, which driver?

924
00:40:00.030 --> 00:40:02.830
We've got two drivers, me and Lee.

925
00:40:03.110 --> 00:40:03.310
Right.

926
00:40:03.450 --> 00:40:05.570
So you have two kids with very different interests.

927
00:40:05.730 --> 00:40:05.990
Yes.

928
00:40:06.190 --> 00:40:07.870
And you're driving them all over town.

929
00:40:07.870 --> 00:40:09.350
It's all I do all weekend.

930
00:40:09.610 --> 00:40:12.390
Half the time on the weekend, you and I trade messages.

931
00:40:12.390 --> 00:40:13.770
You're like, well, I'm back in the car.

932
00:40:14.090 --> 00:40:14.410
Yeah.

933
00:40:15.050 --> 00:40:16.130
All over the city.

934
00:40:17.010 --> 00:40:18.570
That's basically my weekend.

935
00:40:18.790 --> 00:40:21.630
Every weekend is just shuttling kids to things.

936
00:40:22.830 --> 00:40:23.270
But—

937
00:40:23.270 --> 00:40:23.830
I'm sorry.

938
00:40:24.050 --> 00:40:26.890
I think the question is, what do you like to do on the weekends?

939
00:40:27.410 --> 00:40:29.990
Not what you actually do on the weekend.

940
00:40:29.990 --> 00:40:36.250
Right, okay, well, I have been able to carve out, sometimes both on Saturday and Sunday,

941
00:40:36.390 --> 00:40:41.230
but at least one or the other, I sleep in, which I really like.

942
00:40:41.370 --> 00:40:41.670
Yeah.

943
00:40:41.750 --> 00:40:45.030
By sleep in, I mean like 8 a.m., maybe 8.30 if I'm lucky.

944
00:40:45.190 --> 00:40:45.910
That's kind of cool.

945
00:40:46.190 --> 00:40:55.090
And then I get my cup of coffee, and I go upstairs to my reading nook, and I read my book for a long time.

946
00:40:55.090 --> 00:41:00.990
If I get to read during the week, maybe it's only for 15 minutes, but on a weekend,

947
00:41:00.990 --> 00:41:11.930
I create the space where I get to just sink into my cozy chair in my little nook at the top of my stairs with my fabulous chairs and my wallpaper.

948
00:41:13.150 --> 00:41:20.010
This nook—okay, so you've had that house a long time, but y'all, this nook was like that little kind of square off the top of your stairs.

949
00:41:20.150 --> 00:41:20.910
It's like the little landing.

950
00:41:21.130 --> 00:41:22.730
It's next to the linen cabinet.

951
00:41:22.730 --> 00:41:25.550
Yeah, and for years, you were like, wait, I don't know what to do with this space.

952
00:41:25.690 --> 00:41:27.170
I don't know what it is.

953
00:41:27.310 --> 00:41:36.070
It's not big enough to really put too much in it, and then it just kind of became a catch-all a little bit, and then you made it this cozy nook, and lady,

954
00:41:36.270 --> 00:41:37.410
it's brought you so much joy.

955
00:41:37.550 --> 00:41:39.390
It's where I read now, and I love it.

956
00:41:39.410 --> 00:41:41.030
It's where I did all my prep for the play.

957
00:41:41.030 --> 00:41:41.970
I really like it.

958
00:41:42.030 --> 00:41:43.050
It's my quiet spot.

959
00:41:43.470 --> 00:41:50.290
So yeah, that's what I like to do, and I make sure that my weekend, one of the days, sometimes both, includes that.

960
00:41:50.290 --> 00:41:52.910
And I'll sit there from like 8.30 to 10 o'clock.

961
00:41:53.050 --> 00:41:53.810
Oh, wow.

962
00:41:53.930 --> 00:41:54.490
That's amazing.

963
00:41:54.870 --> 00:41:56.450
And then I have to start—

964
00:41:56.450 --> 00:41:56.990
Then you have to start driving.

965
00:41:57.310 --> 00:41:57.490
Yeah.

966
00:41:57.770 --> 00:41:59.810
You have to go drive your clients around town.

967
00:41:59.930 --> 00:42:00.310
Exactly.

968
00:42:00.710 --> 00:42:01.090
All right.

969
00:42:01.290 --> 00:42:02.910
Last call sheet question.

970
00:42:03.290 --> 00:42:05.670
What is your favorite midnight snack?

971
00:42:06.010 --> 00:42:07.390
Well, I think it's cereal.

972
00:42:08.050 --> 00:42:12.790
If I'm going to have a snack, and I'm interpreting midnight snack as just—

973
00:42:12.790 --> 00:42:13.890
Like 9 p.m.

974
00:42:14.170 --> 00:42:14.970
Yeah, exactly.

975
00:42:15.210 --> 00:42:16.570
Like, well, my midnight.

976
00:42:16.810 --> 00:42:18.530
Yeah, we're not up that late anymore.

977
00:42:18.890 --> 00:42:19.970
Pre-bedtime snack.

978
00:42:20.130 --> 00:42:20.270
Yeah.

979
00:42:20.270 --> 00:42:23.530
If I'm going to get hungry and want to eat something, it's going to be cereal.

980
00:42:23.830 --> 00:42:27.190
And currently, I'm into frosted mini-wheats.

981
00:42:27.510 --> 00:42:28.270
Oh, really?

982
00:42:28.450 --> 00:42:28.770
Mm-hmm.

983
00:42:28.770 --> 00:42:29.250
I know.

984
00:42:29.250 --> 00:42:32.910
It used to be Lucky Charms, but now frosted mini-wheats.

985
00:42:33.230 --> 00:42:35.930
Your cereal always has a little punch of sugar in it.

986
00:42:35.930 --> 00:42:36.710
It does.

987
00:42:36.990 --> 00:42:38.410
It packs a punch.

988
00:42:38.790 --> 00:42:43.770
But when I was younger, and I really did eat things at midnight, my two favorites were,

989
00:42:43.950 --> 00:42:49.510
like, a big tub of frozen yogurt or, you know, Ben & Jerry's ice cream.

990
00:42:49.770 --> 00:42:56.490
Or for a very long time, I would watch TV and eat easy cheese on top of wheat thins.

991
00:42:58.920 --> 00:42:59.920
That's hilarious.

992
00:43:00.680 --> 00:43:07.660
Well, lady, all of those answers are so authentically you, and you're really fun to hang out with.

993
00:43:07.880 --> 00:43:08.880
Hey, thanks.

994
00:43:09.120 --> 00:43:10.840
Yeah, you're a good hang, as they say.

995
00:43:11.140 --> 00:43:12.400
Why don't we take a break?

996
00:43:12.400 --> 00:43:17.340
And when we come back, I think we should have an Around the Town and then end with an office

997
00:43:17.340 --> 00:43:18.240
question of the week.

998
00:43:18.360 --> 00:43:18.860
Let's do it.

999
00:43:29.100 --> 00:43:30.080
Sam, we're back.

1000
00:43:30.080 --> 00:43:32.020
How about some Around the Town action?

1001
00:43:32.020 --> 00:43:39.440
Our first message comes from Julia H.

1002
00:43:39.560 --> 00:43:43.580
in Connecticut, who said, Julia and Sarah, who have been friends

1003
00:43:43.580 --> 00:43:49.840
since 2016 when they joined the University of Connecticut marching band as clarinet players,

1004
00:43:50.300 --> 00:43:53.640
celebrated their 28th birthdays over the weekend.

1005
00:43:53.640 --> 00:44:00.760
They hosted a, quote, bob party, where their friends, who are also clarinet players, came dressed as

1006
00:44:00.760 --> 00:44:03.240
characters who would have bob haircuts.

1007
00:44:03.800 --> 00:44:08.680
The party also included pizza and ice cream cake for dessert.

1008
00:44:08.980 --> 00:44:10.300
That is hilarious.

1009
00:44:10.300 --> 00:44:12.140
I have never heard of a bob party.

1010
00:44:12.640 --> 00:44:17.520
